1
00:02:28,481 --> 00:02:31,094
How'd he get to you? Who?

2
00:02:31,118 --> 00:02:33,785
That federal agent, Paul Davies.

3
00:02:38,191 --> 00:02:41,538
He said that it was absolutely
imperative that I plead guilty.

4
00:02:41,562 --> 00:02:44,040
He said that the investigation
he was spearheading

5
00:02:44,064 --> 00:02:45,575
would clear me
and he promised me

6
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:47,443
that I wouldn't
spend a day in prison.

7
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:48,979
What's the matter with you?

8
00:02:49,003 --> 00:02:51,414
He can't promise
that. Nobody can.

9
00:02:51,438 --> 00:02:53,917
The system isn't
made to work that way.

10
00:02:53,941 --> 00:02:56,786
Why didn't you consult
with me before you agreed?

11
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:58,488
He said not to
discuss it with anyone.

12
00:02:58,512 --> 00:03:00,223
He said it was a matter
of national security.

13
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:02,325
Well, maybe that's what he said,

14
00:03:02,349 --> 00:03:04,227
but that's not why you complied.

15
00:03:04,251 --> 00:03:07,029
He promised you an
exclusive, didn't he?

16
00:03:07,053 --> 00:03:08,464
He told you you
could break the story

17
00:03:08,488 --> 00:03:10,466
as soon as his
investigation was over.

18
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:12,668
You weren't thinking
about national security.

19
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:14,892
You were thinking
about a Pulitzer Prize.

20
00:03:16,162 --> 00:03:20,076
You lied, and if this
investigation comes up dry

21
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,179
or loses its funding
or just plain folds,

22
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:25,281
you just made the
biggest mistake of your life.

23
00:03:29,376 --> 00:03:30,753
Yeah, Tyler. Did you find him?

24
00:03:30,777 --> 00:03:32,888
Oh, yeah. Paul Davies?

25
00:03:32,912 --> 00:03:34,557
Yep, and he wants
to talk to you too.

26
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:36,526
Good.

27
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:37,827
Paul Davies,

28
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:40,997
section chief, special projects.

29
00:03:41,021 --> 00:03:42,398
Now, what's this all about?

30
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:50,273
You don't know anything
about a secret investigation?

31
00:03:50,297 --> 00:03:52,776
No, and furthermore,
no one in this office

32
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:55,478
is conducting any
investigation that involves you.

33
00:03:55,502 --> 00:03:58,214
The IDs these guys were flashing

34
00:03:58,238 --> 00:04:00,350
didn't come out of a cereal box.

35
00:04:00,374 --> 00:04:02,418
Did anybody quit
your staff recently?

36
00:04:02,442 --> 00:04:05,588
No one fitting the description
of the men you saw.

37
00:04:05,612 --> 00:04:07,790
Damn. Impersonating
a federal agent's

38
00:04:07,814 --> 00:04:09,459
a serious offense, Mr. Matlock,

39
00:04:09,483 --> 00:04:12,951
and I assure you we will
look into this immediately.

40
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:16,266
I've got a client

41
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:19,169
who just confessed to
murder in open court.

42
00:04:19,193 --> 00:04:21,537
Unless I find who
talked her into that,

43
00:04:21,561 --> 00:04:23,906
she could go to prison
for the rest of her life.

44
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:26,576
And you think the person
that killed this Karen Wyler

45
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,111
is behind all this?

46
00:04:28,135 --> 00:04:29,445
Yes, got to be,

47
00:04:29,469 --> 00:04:30,713
and I think it's one of the men

48
00:04:30,737 --> 00:04:32,314
my office has
been investigating.

49
00:04:32,338 --> 00:04:35,118
Lambert Crawford, for
one. Lambert Crawford?

50
00:04:35,142 --> 00:04:37,287
Senator Lambert Crawford? Yes.

51
00:04:37,311 --> 00:04:39,689
That's who Karen
Wyler was working for

52
00:04:39,713 --> 00:04:40,890
at the time she was murdered.

53
00:04:40,914 --> 00:04:43,326
She believed that
he, Michael Fitzmorris,

54
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:44,994
Robert Irwin... Robert Irwin?

55
00:04:45,018 --> 00:04:46,929
The attorney. And that whiz kid

56
00:04:46,953 --> 00:04:49,932
who ran the senator's
reelection campaign, Jed Palmer.

57
00:04:49,956 --> 00:04:53,003
She told my client, who
works for the newspaper,

58
00:04:53,027 --> 00:04:54,604
that they were up to something

59
00:04:54,628 --> 00:04:57,273
involving a lot of money
and a lot of secrecy.

60
00:04:57,297 --> 00:05:00,310
Now, doesn't it stand to reason
that's why she was murdered?

61
00:05:00,334 --> 00:05:02,300
What kind of proof did she have?

62
00:05:03,937 --> 00:05:04,969
She didn't.

63
00:05:07,808 --> 00:05:10,142
And neither do I.

64
00:05:12,012 --> 00:05:15,057
He told her she'd be helping
her country, Your Honor.

65
00:05:15,081 --> 00:05:17,827
She was tricked into
making that confession.

66
00:05:17,851 --> 00:05:21,231
Your Honor, I was approached
and fooled by these same men.

67
00:05:21,255 --> 00:05:22,498
My client is innocent.

68
00:05:22,522 --> 00:05:25,735
I wanna clear her of these
charges as soon as possible,

69
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:29,806
but I need the time to find
these phony federal agents.

70
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:31,741
Your Honor, I'd
like a continuance.

71
00:05:31,765 --> 00:05:33,631
Denied. Next suggestion.

72
00:05:35,069 --> 00:05:37,113
Then I'll move for a mistrial.

73
00:05:37,137 --> 00:05:39,571
I'll deny that too.

74
00:05:48,748 --> 00:05:50,060
How'd it go?

75
00:05:50,084 --> 00:05:52,062
He shouldn't be
removed from the bench.

76
00:05:52,086 --> 00:05:53,930
He should be removed
from the planet.

77
00:05:53,954 --> 00:05:56,332
That well, huh? Cheer up.

78
00:05:56,356 --> 00:05:58,801
Remember the car that
followed Michelle and Jed Palmer

79
00:05:58,825 --> 00:06:00,336
to his apartment,
the one that belonged

80
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:02,072
to those alleged G-men? Yeah.

81
00:06:02,096 --> 00:06:03,639
I traced the license
plate and found out

82
00:06:03,663 --> 00:06:04,941
who it's registered to. Who?

83
00:06:04,965 --> 00:06:06,142
The Leyland Group.

84
00:06:06,166 --> 00:06:07,310
Who's the Leyland Group?

85
00:06:07,334 --> 00:06:09,345
It's some kind of
giant holding company,

86
00:06:09,369 --> 00:06:12,815
but the most interesting
thing here is the legal counsel

87
00:06:12,839 --> 00:06:14,584
that they've retained
here in Washington.

88
00:06:14,608 --> 00:06:16,219
Who?

89
00:06:16,243 --> 00:06:18,477
Robert Irwin.

90
00:06:19,679 --> 00:06:22,725
Oh.

91
00:06:22,749 --> 00:06:25,661
How about getting
together with Jed Palmer?

92
00:06:25,685 --> 00:06:29,988
See what he knows about this
Leyland Group and Robert Irwin.

93
00:06:34,828 --> 00:06:36,439
Is there a problem?

94
00:06:36,463 --> 00:06:38,596
No. No problem.

95
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:48,272
Coming.

96
00:06:50,511 --> 00:06:51,688
Hello.

97
00:06:51,712 --> 00:06:54,491
Hi.

98
00:06:54,515 --> 00:06:56,860
Welcome back.

99
00:06:56,884 --> 00:06:58,628
Come on in.

100
00:06:58,652 --> 00:07:01,097
I've got some espresso.
It'll be ready in just a minute.

101
00:07:01,121 --> 00:07:02,732
Oh, no. None for me, thank you.

102
00:07:02,756 --> 00:07:05,334
No. It's no problem.

103
00:07:05,358 --> 00:07:08,104
I need to talk to you.

104
00:07:08,128 --> 00:07:10,439
I've got nothing
against talking.

105
00:07:10,463 --> 00:07:12,742
It comes in, uh, pretty
handy sometimes.

106
00:07:12,766 --> 00:07:14,699
You never know
where it might lead.

107
00:07:17,738 --> 00:07:20,249
I wasn't exactly
forthcoming with you last time

108
00:07:20,273 --> 00:07:22,351
about who I am.

109
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:24,253
You're not Michelle Thomas?

110
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:26,789
I am, but I'm also an attorney.

111
00:07:26,813 --> 00:07:28,913
I work with Ben Matlock.

112
00:07:30,884 --> 00:07:32,728
Defending Melinda Stuart?

113
00:07:32,752 --> 00:07:34,452
That's right.

114
00:07:39,059 --> 00:07:40,903
You're very good.

115
00:07:40,927 --> 00:07:42,338
Thanks.

116
00:07:42,362 --> 00:07:47,910
You, uh... You set me up.

117
00:07:47,934 --> 00:07:49,478
Set you up? Yeah.

118
00:07:49,502 --> 00:07:52,003
You had me completely fooled.

119
00:08:00,013 --> 00:08:01,557
I didn't lie to you.

120
00:08:01,581 --> 00:08:03,701
No, but you kept
certain things from me.

121
00:08:10,523 --> 00:08:14,003
When I look at you, I see
a bright, interesting woman.

122
00:08:14,027 --> 00:08:16,806
Someone I can relate
to. Someone I can...

123
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:18,407
be comfortable with.

124
00:08:18,431 --> 00:08:20,932
A woman I can spend
special time with.

125
00:08:22,269 --> 00:08:24,680
You set me up.

126
00:08:24,704 --> 00:08:26,082
What did you want from me?

127
00:08:26,106 --> 00:08:28,266
Just someone to
answer your questions?

128
00:08:29,642 --> 00:08:31,087
I'm really sorry.

129
00:08:31,111 --> 00:08:34,189
I apologize.

130
00:08:34,213 --> 00:08:36,359
But I need to ask
you a couple more.

131
00:08:37,984 --> 00:08:40,796
Why not?

132
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:42,965
Ever hear of the Leyland Group?

133
00:08:42,989 --> 00:08:44,933
Sure. It's some
big conglomerate.

134
00:08:44,957 --> 00:08:46,769
Principal interests are oil.

135
00:08:46,793 --> 00:08:48,337
Headquarters in
Houston, I think.

136
00:08:48,361 --> 00:08:52,375
Ask Michael Fitzmorris.
He used to work for them.

137
00:08:52,399 --> 00:08:54,544
What's Robert Irwin's
connection to them?

138
00:08:54,568 --> 00:08:56,446
I'm afraid you'd
have to ask him that.

139
00:08:56,470 --> 00:08:58,848
What about his
connection to Karen Wyler?

140
00:08:58,872 --> 00:09:00,482
He ever have an affair with her?

141
00:09:00,506 --> 00:09:02,285
Again, you'd have to ask him.

142
00:09:02,309 --> 00:09:05,188
What about Crawford?
You'd have to ask him too.

143
00:09:05,212 --> 00:09:07,178
What about you?

144
00:09:08,648 --> 00:09:11,008
Of what possible
relevance is any of this?

145
00:09:12,419 --> 00:09:14,897
Whoever actually killed
her was very familiar

146
00:09:14,921 --> 00:09:16,766
with the burglar alarm
system in her apartment,

147
00:09:16,790 --> 00:09:19,924
and I imagine very
familiar with her.

148
00:09:21,328 --> 00:09:23,172
Which rules me out.

149
00:09:23,196 --> 00:09:25,974
I was at the Brazilian
embassy that whole evening

150
00:09:25,998 --> 00:09:28,644
with 150 other people.

151
00:09:28,668 --> 00:09:31,781
She told Melinda
that your relationship

152
00:09:31,805 --> 00:09:34,984
with Senator Crawford went
beyond being a media consultant.

153
00:09:35,008 --> 00:09:36,853
What's that supposed to mean?

154
00:09:36,877 --> 00:09:38,777
I was hoping you'd tell me.

155
00:09:41,348 --> 00:09:46,150
I wish I could, but I can't.

156
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:49,465
Too bad you can't ask Karen.

157
00:09:49,489 --> 00:09:52,690
Yeah. It's too bad.

158
00:09:54,861 --> 00:10:00,342
Well, thanks for the talk.

159
00:10:00,366 --> 00:10:02,745
Anytime, Michelle.

160
00:10:02,769 --> 00:10:04,914
And, uh, listen.

161
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:09,418
Next time you have a
question to ask me, just ask.

162
00:10:09,442 --> 00:10:11,475
Don't set me up.

163
00:10:24,391 --> 00:10:27,169
Well, if it's information you
were looking for, Mr. Matlock,

164
00:10:27,193 --> 00:10:29,972
you have definitely
come to the right place.

165
00:10:29,996 --> 00:10:32,241
You know, many people,
my husband included,

166
00:10:32,265 --> 00:10:35,845
regard me as nothing but
a flighty, garrulous old fool.

167
00:10:35,869 --> 00:10:38,781
Well, not only do I
have eyes in my head,

168
00:10:38,805 --> 00:10:40,716
I have a brain as well.

169
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:44,887
Oh, I could tell that
the instant I met you.

170
00:10:44,911 --> 00:10:48,057
In fact, I... I said to
Michelle, I... I said,

171
00:10:48,081 --> 00:10:52,094
"Michelle, there
is a smart woman."

172
00:10:52,118 --> 00:10:53,629
You know what she said?

173
00:10:53,653 --> 00:10:55,597
What? "I know it."

174
00:10:55,621 --> 00:10:58,000
Oh, she is such
a wonderful girl.

175
00:10:58,024 --> 00:10:59,168
Oh, isn't she?

176
00:10:59,192 --> 00:11:02,237
You are a very, very
lucky man. I know. I know.

177
00:11:02,261 --> 00:11:05,808
Now, what do you know about
your husband's connection

178
00:11:05,832 --> 00:11:07,977
with the Leyland Group?

179
00:11:08,001 --> 00:11:10,146
More than I'm supposed
to, I can tell you that.

180
00:11:10,170 --> 00:11:12,247
You know, I knew
something was up

181
00:11:12,271 --> 00:11:14,616
when Robert started to
lock the door of his study

182
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:16,585
whenever they'd
call him at home.

183
00:11:16,609 --> 00:11:18,220
Well, I mean, that only served

184
00:11:18,244 --> 00:11:20,455
to pique my desire
to learn more.

185
00:11:20,479 --> 00:11:24,060
Oh. So... did you?

186
00:11:24,084 --> 00:11:27,363
Mm. Are you sure that you
won't have one of these?

187
00:11:27,387 --> 00:11:30,332
They are quite delicious.
Oh, no, thank you, Miss Irwin.

188
00:11:30,356 --> 00:11:32,134
Oh, please, please.

189
00:11:32,158 --> 00:11:33,569
Clarissa.

190
00:11:33,593 --> 00:11:35,771
You know, whenever
anyone calls me Mrs. Irwin,

191
00:11:35,795 --> 00:11:37,439
I tend to look over my shoulder

192
00:11:37,463 --> 00:11:39,942
expecting to see
Robert's mother.

193
00:11:39,966 --> 00:11:41,577
Which is a little disconcerting,

194
00:11:41,601 --> 00:11:44,580
as she died seven years ago.

195
00:11:48,174 --> 00:11:51,253
Did you, uh...?
Did you find out?

196
00:11:51,277 --> 00:11:52,654
About what?

197
00:11:52,678 --> 00:11:53,755
The Leyland Group.

198
00:11:53,779 --> 00:11:55,524
Oh, the Leyland Group.

199
00:11:55,548 --> 00:11:57,426
Excuse me. Mr. Matlock,

200
00:11:57,450 --> 00:11:59,562
you have a phone
call from a Mr. Davies.

201
00:11:59,586 --> 00:12:00,863
Which one?

202
00:12:00,887 --> 00:12:02,931
All I know is he says
it's an emergency.

203
00:12:02,955 --> 00:12:06,224
Oh, please go ahead
and take it. I'm in no hurry.

204
00:12:09,729 --> 00:12:13,198
<i>Uno más.</i> Ahem.

205
00:12:17,003 --> 00:12:19,236
This is Ben Matlock.

206
00:12:22,042 --> 00:12:24,253
Hello?

207
00:12:24,277 --> 00:12:26,110
Hello?

208
00:13:01,247 --> 00:13:03,826
Hey, Clarissa!

209
00:13:25,037 --> 00:13:27,516
I'm telling you there's
a conspiracy afoot,

210
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:30,041
and my client is innocent.

211
00:13:31,411 --> 00:13:34,190
Is this the same client who
confessed in open court?

212
00:13:34,214 --> 00:13:36,492
She was tricked into
that by the same people

213
00:13:36,516 --> 00:13:39,116
who kidnapped Clarissa Irwin.

214
00:13:40,587 --> 00:13:42,498
Lieutenant, what
is this all about?

215
00:13:42,522 --> 00:13:45,300
Sorry to bother you,
but Mr. Matlock here

216
00:13:45,324 --> 00:13:46,768
claims your wife
has been kidnapped.

217
00:13:46,792 --> 00:13:47,837
Kidnapped?

218
00:13:47,861 --> 00:13:49,972
Do you have any idea
where she is right now?

219
00:13:49,996 --> 00:13:52,741
I know precisely
where she is right now.

220
00:13:52,765 --> 00:13:56,946
She's down in Texas at a
spa called, uh, the Springs.

221
00:13:56,970 --> 00:13:59,047
Call the place and get
Mrs. Irwin on the line.

222
00:13:59,071 --> 00:14:02,684
I know I had lunch
with her, at least tried to.

223
00:14:02,708 --> 00:14:04,453
I really don't
have time for this.

224
00:14:04,477 --> 00:14:07,189
Call the maitre d' at Rex's.
- We did.

225
00:14:07,213 --> 00:14:08,557
He says while you
were on the phone,

226
00:14:08,581 --> 00:14:11,227
Mrs. Irwin got up and
left with two gentlemen.

227
00:14:11,251 --> 00:14:14,663
Those gentlemen are
impersonating federal agents.

228
00:14:14,687 --> 00:14:16,053
Lieutenant, line one.

229
00:14:20,460 --> 00:14:23,739
Mrs. Irwin? Hello.

230
00:14:23,763 --> 00:14:26,130
This is James Norris from
the Washington, D.C. police.

231
00:14:27,167 --> 00:14:28,911
Fine, thank you.

232
00:14:28,935 --> 00:14:32,448
More to the point, how
are you, Mrs. Irwin?

233
00:14:32,472 --> 00:14:34,950
Oh. Sorry, uh, Clarissa.

234
00:14:34,974 --> 00:14:36,040
Uh.

235
00:14:37,677 --> 00:14:39,121
Clarissa?

236
00:14:39,145 --> 00:14:41,590
Ben Matlock. What in
the world's going on?

237
00:14:41,614 --> 00:14:44,026
I do apologize for getting
up and leaving like that,

238
00:14:44,050 --> 00:14:46,428
but I'd entirely forgotten
that I booked myself in

239
00:14:46,452 --> 00:14:47,829
for a stay down here.

240
00:14:47,853 --> 00:14:51,533
And the spa is just
dreadful about no-shows.

241
00:14:51,557 --> 00:14:54,136
I'm sorry, Ben. I have to go.

242
00:14:55,995 --> 00:14:58,107
Whatever it is
you're trying to pull,

243
00:14:58,131 --> 00:14:59,742
I advise you
not to try it again.

244
00:14:59,766 --> 00:15:01,911
Thank you for coming down
and helping us clear this up.

245
00:15:01,935 --> 00:15:04,446
My apologies.

246
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:05,736
Okay.

247
00:15:08,708 --> 00:15:10,553
- The Springs?
- Yeah.

248
00:15:10,577 --> 00:15:13,289
Some kind of spa in Texas.

249
00:15:13,313 --> 00:15:15,057
Well, what am I supposed
to do when I find her?

250
00:15:15,081 --> 00:15:16,525
Talk to her.

251
00:15:16,549 --> 00:15:18,760
We've got to find out what
her husband's connection is

252
00:15:18,784 --> 00:15:21,263
with the Leyland Group.
Now, she was kidnapped?

253
00:15:21,287 --> 00:15:23,466
I thought so. Well, if she was,

254
00:15:23,490 --> 00:15:25,968
what if the people who did it
don't want her to talk to me?

255
00:15:25,992 --> 00:15:29,171
Find a way. BOTH: You always do.

256
00:15:29,195 --> 00:15:31,440
<i>Their identification
looked real.</i>

257
00:15:31,464 --> 00:15:33,376
It looked real.

258
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:36,545
I honestly believed that
they were federal agents.

259
00:15:36,569 --> 00:15:39,248
So I did what they asked me to.

260
00:15:39,272 --> 00:15:41,784
I confessed.

261
00:15:41,808 --> 00:15:46,054
Melinda, did you
shoot Karen Wyler?

262
00:15:46,078 --> 00:15:48,023
No.

263
00:15:50,684 --> 00:15:52,795
That's all, thank you.

264
00:15:52,819 --> 00:15:54,863
Just tell me this, Miss Stuart.

265
00:15:54,887 --> 00:15:57,566
Were you lying the other
day, or were you lying just now,

266
00:15:57,590 --> 00:15:58,834
or were you lying both times

267
00:15:58,858 --> 00:16:01,837
and have yet
another tale to tell us?

268
00:16:01,861 --> 00:16:05,523
Um... Approach the
bench, Your Honor?

269
00:16:13,539 --> 00:16:17,252
Your Honor, I submit

270
00:16:17,276 --> 00:16:20,355
that you're impugning
the credibility of my client

271
00:16:20,379 --> 00:16:23,513
with your every word,
gesture, and expression.

272
00:16:24,951 --> 00:16:27,596
And if you continue,
I'll have no choice

273
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:31,867
but to move to have Your
Honor disqualified for prejudice.

274
00:16:31,891 --> 00:16:35,037
Lack of impartiality is probably
the most serious charge

275
00:16:35,061 --> 00:16:38,474
that can be leveled against
someone in my position.

276
00:16:38,498 --> 00:16:42,078
Are you certain that your
observations are not colored

277
00:16:42,102 --> 00:16:44,680
by your own
partiality, Mr. Matlock?

278
00:16:44,704 --> 00:16:48,417
Your Honor, I'm
supposed to be partial.

279
00:16:48,441 --> 00:16:49,873
I'm the defense.

280
00:16:51,610 --> 00:16:54,657
Mr. Hobbs, are you aware

281
00:16:54,681 --> 00:16:57,626
of any demonstrations
of prejudice on my part?

282
00:16:57,650 --> 00:17:00,395
No, Your Honor. No.

283
00:17:00,419 --> 00:17:03,899
Then I suggest not only that
you reconsider your options,

284
00:17:03,923 --> 00:17:05,267
Mr. Matlock,

285
00:17:05,291 --> 00:17:08,504
but that you bear
this in mind as well:

286
00:17:08,528 --> 00:17:11,374
I have never been
removed for cause,

287
00:17:11,398 --> 00:17:15,499
but I have cited many,
many lawyers for contempt.

288
00:17:18,237 --> 00:17:20,270
Do I make myself clear?

289
00:17:21,340 --> 00:17:22,806
Abundantly.

290
00:17:26,746 --> 00:17:29,391
Should I repeat the
question, Miss Stuart?

291
00:17:29,415 --> 00:17:32,160
I lied before, I admit that,

292
00:17:32,184 --> 00:17:34,796
but I did not kill Karen Wyler.

293
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:37,967
Let me point out that
it doesn't excuse her

294
00:17:37,991 --> 00:17:40,536
because she was lying before...
- Mr. Matlock,

295
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:42,438
I asked Miss
Stuart the question.

296
00:17:42,462 --> 00:17:45,507
Your Honor, I am this
woman's defense attorney.

297
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:47,576
I have the right to speak.

298
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:49,678
When she confessed,
she was not under oath.

299
00:17:49,702 --> 00:17:51,246
She is under oath now.

300
00:17:51,270 --> 00:17:54,316
Her statement that she
did not kill Karen Wyler

301
00:17:54,340 --> 00:17:55,651
is for the record!

302
00:17:55,675 --> 00:17:57,908
Mr. Matlock, sit down!

303
00:18:09,388 --> 00:18:11,233
Believe me, Mr. Davenport,

304
00:18:11,257 --> 00:18:13,869
if discretion and results
are what you want,

305
00:18:13,893 --> 00:18:15,604
the Springs is unsurpassed.

306
00:18:15,628 --> 00:18:18,540
Good, because that's
exactly what I'd heard.

307
00:18:18,564 --> 00:18:21,777
And we are particularly
adept at meeting the needs

308
00:18:21,801 --> 00:18:27,015
of people who
are... Well-heeled?

309
00:18:27,039 --> 00:18:30,886
Well, since we seem to
understand each other,

310
00:18:30,910 --> 00:18:34,022
what exactly is
your wife's problem?

311
00:18:34,046 --> 00:18:38,694
She is, uh... fat.

312
00:18:38,718 --> 00:18:41,797
Well, a weight problem
is no problem for us.

313
00:18:41,821 --> 00:18:43,865
Look, why don't I
take you on a little tour

314
00:18:43,889 --> 00:18:46,201
and I'll show you how we deal
with our food dependencies.

315
00:18:46,225 --> 00:18:49,371
As soon as I answer this call.

316
00:18:49,395 --> 00:18:51,039
Please, why don't
you have a seat outside

317
00:18:51,063 --> 00:18:54,098
and I'll be right with you.
Thank you very much.

318
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:58,046
Yes, this is the manager.

319
00:18:58,070 --> 00:18:59,848
No, we have not
received those orders yet,

320
00:18:59,872 --> 00:19:02,440
and I would like for you
to check on that for me.

321
00:19:56,829 --> 00:19:58,373
Mr. Davenport?

322
00:20:00,299 --> 00:20:01,732
Hey!

323
00:20:43,342 --> 00:20:46,355
<i>I don't know. I just...</i>

324
00:20:46,379 --> 00:20:48,890
<i>I just locked myself out.</i>

325
00:20:48,914 --> 00:20:51,293
Tyler...

326
00:20:51,317 --> 00:20:54,195
You've gotta get back in
there and get Clarissa out.

327
00:20:54,219 --> 00:20:55,564
Whoever we're dealing with

328
00:20:55,588 --> 00:20:58,989
might decide to make
her stay permanent.

329
00:21:00,125 --> 00:21:01,236
Do you understand?

330
00:21:01,260 --> 00:21:03,972
Okay, okay.

331
00:21:03,996 --> 00:21:06,429
I'll be in touch.

332
00:21:08,801 --> 00:21:11,680
Clarissa Irwin is
at that spa in Texas

333
00:21:11,704 --> 00:21:13,349
under heavy sedation.

334
00:21:13,373 --> 00:21:16,986
The Springs is owned
by the Leyland Group.

335
00:21:17,010 --> 00:21:19,054
I'm gonna listen to the
tapes that Melinda made

336
00:21:19,078 --> 00:21:20,456
of her interviews with Karen

337
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:21,890
to see if there's
anything on them

338
00:21:21,914 --> 00:21:24,526
that might tell us something
Melinda may have overlooked.

339
00:21:24,550 --> 00:21:27,562
As usual, you're two
steps ahead of me.

340
00:21:27,586 --> 00:21:28,797
Where are you going?

341
00:21:28,821 --> 00:21:30,699
Once you get a fire started,

342
00:21:30,723 --> 00:21:34,903
you've gotta stir the pot.

343
00:21:34,927 --> 00:21:37,138
There's always work to be done.

344
00:21:37,162 --> 00:21:39,507
That's why I love this job.

345
00:21:39,531 --> 00:21:41,509
This country never stands still.

346
00:21:41,533 --> 00:21:43,345
It's always moving.

347
00:21:43,369 --> 00:21:44,512
Growing.

348
00:21:44,536 --> 00:21:46,949
Changing.

349
00:21:46,973 --> 00:21:50,418
I've helped clean
up after World War II.

350
00:21:50,442 --> 00:21:54,122
I've done my damndest
to prevent World War III.

351
00:21:54,146 --> 00:21:57,459
I've come through
missile crises, oil crises,

352
00:21:57,483 --> 00:21:59,895
overseen aid to our schools,

353
00:21:59,919 --> 00:22:02,030
aid to our farmers.

354
00:22:02,054 --> 00:22:05,000
Never the same thing twice.

355
00:22:05,024 --> 00:22:06,602
I like to think that means

356
00:22:06,626 --> 00:22:08,470
that we must've been
doing something right.

357
00:22:08,494 --> 00:22:10,205
Yeah.

358
00:22:10,229 --> 00:22:13,942
I, uh, I... I admire
people in public office.

359
00:22:13,966 --> 00:22:15,811
I really do.

360
00:22:15,835 --> 00:22:18,046
Uh, didn't you become chairman

361
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:20,149
of the Senate committee

362
00:22:20,173 --> 00:22:22,684
that polices the activities
of big oil companies

363
00:22:22,708 --> 00:22:24,887
about the time
of the oil crisis?

364
00:22:24,911 --> 00:22:27,455
That's right. I have
reason to believe

365
00:22:27,479 --> 00:22:29,892
that the Leyland Group...

366
00:22:29,916 --> 00:22:32,427
Y-You're familiar with the
Leyland Group, are you?

367
00:22:32,451 --> 00:22:34,263
Yes, of course.
It's a conglomerate

368
00:22:34,287 --> 00:22:36,498
which owns several
oil companies.

369
00:22:36,522 --> 00:22:39,501
I have reason to believe
that the Leyland Group

370
00:22:39,525 --> 00:22:43,126
is somehow involved
in Karen Wyler's murder.

371
00:22:44,897 --> 00:22:47,408
Why would they want
to murder my secretary?

372
00:22:47,432 --> 00:22:49,578
I don't know. I don't know,

373
00:22:49,602 --> 00:22:52,648
but I do know that
Michael Fitzmorris,

374
00:22:52,672 --> 00:22:55,372
your legislative assistant,
used to work for them.

375
00:22:57,710 --> 00:22:59,488
Mike, step in here, please.

376
00:22:59,512 --> 00:23:01,289
It could be that it's
just a coincidence,

377
00:23:01,313 --> 00:23:05,494
or it could be that he's
some sort of watchdog

378
00:23:05,518 --> 00:23:07,529
who's supposed to make sure

379
00:23:07,553 --> 00:23:10,899
that a certain elected
official holds up his end

380
00:23:10,923 --> 00:23:14,836
of whatever deal was struck
with this Leyland Group.

381
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:17,773
I don't think I like what
you're implying, Mr. Matlock.

382
00:23:17,797 --> 00:23:19,207
What's going on?

383
00:23:19,231 --> 00:23:23,178
I was just sounding out a couple
of ideas on the senator here,

384
00:23:23,202 --> 00:23:27,115
asking him if, uh, he
thinks maybe a person

385
00:23:27,139 --> 00:23:29,851
who thought he
was facing retirement

386
00:23:29,875 --> 00:23:31,420
might've gotten a little greedy

387
00:23:31,444 --> 00:23:33,455
and allowed himself
to be bought off.

388
00:23:33,479 --> 00:23:37,725
After all, 40 years is a long
time to remain idealistic,

389
00:23:37,749 --> 00:23:40,195
isn't it, senator?

390
00:23:40,219 --> 00:23:42,364
What the hell are
you suggesting?

391
00:23:42,388 --> 00:23:43,865
Oh, nothing. Nothing,

392
00:23:43,889 --> 00:23:45,968
just shooting off my mouth.

393
00:23:45,992 --> 00:23:49,126
I guess it comes from
sitting in a courtroom all day.

394
00:23:52,164 --> 00:23:56,678
Oh, uh, uh, I spoke
with that security guard

395
00:23:56,702 --> 00:23:59,714
about, uh, your being up here

396
00:23:59,738 --> 00:24:02,884
working with the senator
at the time of the murder.

397
00:24:02,908 --> 00:24:06,221
He said that he
saw you around 8,

398
00:24:06,245 --> 00:24:09,557
and the murder occurred
in Karen's apartment

399
00:24:09,581 --> 00:24:10,747
around 9.

400
00:25:30,262 --> 00:25:31,939
Mrs. Irwin?

401
00:25:31,963 --> 00:25:33,708
Mrs. Irwin, wake up.

402
00:25:33,732 --> 00:25:35,843
You're being released.

403
00:25:35,867 --> 00:25:37,579
You can change
in the truck. Truck?

404
00:25:37,603 --> 00:25:39,247
Oh, yeah, and we're
all out of wheelchairs,

405
00:25:39,271 --> 00:25:42,383
so you... You can ride in this.

406
00:25:42,407 --> 00:25:44,808
There you go.

407
00:25:54,586 --> 00:25:56,319
Truck?

408
00:26:26,818 --> 00:26:29,064
Wait a minute. Wa...
Hey, wait a minute.

409
00:26:29,088 --> 00:26:30,966
Let me give you
a hand there, pal.

410
00:26:30,990 --> 00:26:32,934
Let's go.

411
00:26:34,927 --> 00:26:37,939
Gee, what you got
in here? A dead cow?

412
00:26:37,963 --> 00:26:42,377
That's it. I'll be
insulted no more.

413
00:26:57,149 --> 00:26:59,327
Wait a minute! Hey!

414
00:26:59,351 --> 00:27:00,595
What? What?

415
00:27:00,619 --> 00:27:02,797
Somebody just drove
out of here with Mrs. Irwin.

416
00:27:10,963 --> 00:27:13,808
<i>So when Congressman
Levin didn't get re-elected,</i>

417
00:27:13,832 --> 00:27:16,378
<i>he called Lambert's office
and got him to hire me.</i>

418
00:27:16,402 --> 00:27:17,979
<i>Did you sleep with the senator?</i>

419
00:27:18,003 --> 00:27:20,081
<i>Maybe I did, maybe I didn't.</i>

420
00:27:20,105 --> 00:27:23,752
<i>Karen, I need details.</i>

421
00:27:23,776 --> 00:27:26,555
<i>Telling you all this
is making me feel...</i>

422
00:27:26,579 --> 00:27:28,890
<i>- Maybe this is a mistake.
- No, it's not.</i>

423
00:27:28,914 --> 00:27:31,348
<i>Please, keep going.</i>

424
00:27:33,385 --> 00:27:36,164
<i>What about Jed Palmer?</i>

425
00:27:37,322 --> 00:27:40,102
You get anything?
Anything usable?

426
00:27:40,126 --> 00:27:43,059
Not so far, but maybe.

427
00:27:45,664 --> 00:27:49,010
<i>He can make you feel
like somebody special.</i>

428
00:27:49,034 --> 00:27:51,480
<i>He said I was a bright,
interesting woman.</i>

429
00:27:51,504 --> 00:27:54,316
<i>Somebody he could relate to.</i>

430
00:27:54,340 --> 00:27:58,086
<i>Somebody he could
be comfortable with.</i>

431
00:27:58,110 --> 00:28:01,756
<i>Somebody he could
spend special time with.</i>

432
00:28:01,780 --> 00:28:04,326
<i>Ha-ha. Well, I
guess it was special.</i>

433
00:28:04,350 --> 00:28:06,628
<i>He got what he wanted.</i>

434
00:28:06,652 --> 00:28:09,764
<i>I, uh, I slept with
him for a while,</i>

435
00:28:09,788 --> 00:28:11,433
<i>then it was over,</i>

436
00:28:11,457 --> 00:28:14,101
<i>and on the off chance
that there are still women</i>

437
00:28:14,125 --> 00:28:15,737
<i>in this town he
hasn't slept with,</i>

438
00:28:15,761 --> 00:28:17,305
<i>somebody ought to warn them.</i>

439
00:28:17,329 --> 00:28:21,109
<i>He's a user and he's got
more lines than Shakespeare.</i>

440
00:28:21,133 --> 00:28:22,999
I don't believe it.

441
00:28:24,136 --> 00:28:25,847
He makes me feel like a louse

442
00:28:25,871 --> 00:28:27,349
for not being totally
honest with him,

443
00:28:27,373 --> 00:28:30,552
and then he turns right around
and looks me straight in the eye

444
00:28:30,576 --> 00:28:32,220
and gives me the same line.

445
00:28:32,244 --> 00:28:33,521
Boy, he's in for it.

446
00:28:33,545 --> 00:28:35,390
Wait a minute. You
can't go over there.

447
00:28:35,414 --> 00:28:37,926
He might be the
one who killed Karen.

448
00:28:37,950 --> 00:28:40,717
Then he's really in for it.

449
00:28:44,690 --> 00:28:47,869
I think we can
freeze-frame right here

450
00:28:47,893 --> 00:28:49,537
on the kid's eyes.

451
00:28:49,561 --> 00:28:52,006
Super the client's name here

452
00:28:52,030 --> 00:28:53,575
and have a voiceover say,

453
00:28:53,599 --> 00:28:55,477
"Let's start tomorrow today.

454
00:28:55,501 --> 00:28:57,244
Vote for Hal Rice."
That kind of thing.

455
00:28:57,268 --> 00:28:59,102
Yeah?

456
00:29:01,573 --> 00:29:03,272
I need to see you, Mr. Palmer.

457
00:29:04,677 --> 00:29:07,310
Take five. Okay.

458
00:29:11,116 --> 00:29:13,728
I heard what Karen
Wyler said about you.

459
00:29:13,752 --> 00:29:16,965
I listened to a tape of an
interview she did with Melinda.

460
00:29:16,989 --> 00:29:18,722
Guess what.

461
00:29:19,859 --> 00:29:22,637
She said we had an affair.

462
00:29:22,661 --> 00:29:24,639
You could say that, yeah.

463
00:29:24,663 --> 00:29:27,175
I also checked with
the Brazilian embassy.

464
00:29:27,199 --> 00:29:28,576
Guess what. Now, wait a minute.

465
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:30,678
I was there. You
arrived around 10.

466
00:29:30,702 --> 00:29:33,315
Karen was murdered around 9.

467
00:29:33,339 --> 00:29:35,672
Are you saying I'm a suspect?

468
00:29:36,575 --> 00:29:38,519
You, Senator Crawford,

469
00:29:38,543 --> 00:29:40,543
Michael Fitzmorris,
and Robert Irwin.

470
00:29:43,182 --> 00:29:47,528
Michelle, I didn't kill her.

471
00:29:47,552 --> 00:29:51,232
Just like you didn't
sleep with her?

472
00:29:51,256 --> 00:29:54,335
All right, I lied. I was
trying to be discreet.

473
00:29:54,359 --> 00:29:57,527
In my profession,
it's called perjury.

474
00:29:58,998 --> 00:30:02,577
I just didn't want to say
something that might

475
00:30:02,601 --> 00:30:06,903
jeopardize our... Our
budding relationship.

476
00:30:07,940 --> 00:30:10,300
Budding relationship? Mmm-hm.

477
00:30:13,112 --> 00:30:15,957
You say I'm a bright,
interesting woman.

478
00:30:15,981 --> 00:30:17,259
Someone you can relate to.

479
00:30:17,283 --> 00:30:19,795
Someone you can
be comfortable with.

480
00:30:19,819 --> 00:30:21,859
That's the same line
you used on Karen.

481
00:30:23,555 --> 00:30:27,502
All you were trying to do
was get me where you got her.

482
00:30:27,526 --> 00:30:29,805
Truth and lies are
the same with you,

483
00:30:29,829 --> 00:30:31,773
something you
just edit in or out.

484
00:30:31,797 --> 00:30:33,341
You either wave it
around like a flag,

485
00:30:33,365 --> 00:30:34,810
or you shove it in a drawer,

486
00:30:34,834 --> 00:30:38,101
whichever happens to suit your
professional or sexual needs.

487
00:30:39,638 --> 00:30:41,683
One woman is dead

488
00:30:41,707 --> 00:30:43,618
and another faces
life imprisonment,

489
00:30:43,642 --> 00:30:45,320
and all you're worried about

490
00:30:45,344 --> 00:30:48,311
is our budding relationship?

491
00:30:51,450 --> 00:30:55,518
Well, don't, 'cause
there ain't one.

492
00:31:04,929 --> 00:31:07,008
Oh.

493
00:31:07,032 --> 00:31:09,511
I feel like I've been
asleep for days.

494
00:31:09,535 --> 00:31:10,811
You have been.

495
00:31:10,835 --> 00:31:13,848
There's a thermos
of coffee on the floor.

496
00:31:13,872 --> 00:31:15,850
Oh, you dear thing.

497
00:31:15,874 --> 00:31:17,352
What foresight.

498
00:31:17,376 --> 00:31:19,787
Any idea why your husband
would do this to you?

499
00:31:19,811 --> 00:31:22,357
I know precisely
why he did this to me.

500
00:31:22,381 --> 00:31:26,094
It was because of what
I was about to tell Ben.

501
00:31:26,118 --> 00:31:27,261
What?

502
00:31:27,285 --> 00:31:29,631
Sorrel Flats.

503
00:31:29,655 --> 00:31:31,966
Who is Sorrel Flats?

504
00:31:31,990 --> 00:31:35,603
I thought it was
a who at first too.

505
00:31:35,627 --> 00:31:39,040
To be honest, I thought it
was one of Robert's mistresses,

506
00:31:39,064 --> 00:31:42,110
something he was hiding
from me out of etiquette.

507
00:31:42,134 --> 00:31:44,879
But finally I realized
he was hiding it from me

508
00:31:44,903 --> 00:31:47,916
because he really
didn't want me to know.

509
00:31:47,940 --> 00:31:51,052
But, of course, I made it
my business to find out.

510
00:31:51,076 --> 00:31:53,754
He must've known I would.

511
00:31:53,778 --> 00:31:57,025
Mrs. Irwin, what
is Sorrel Flats?

512
00:31:57,049 --> 00:31:58,592
Oh, please, please.
Call me Clarissa.

513
00:31:58,616 --> 00:32:00,728
Whenever anybody
calls me Mrs. Irwin,

514
00:32:00,752 --> 00:32:02,230
I feel so terribly
uncomfortable.

515
00:32:02,254 --> 00:32:06,901
Clarissa, what is Sorrel Flats?

516
00:32:06,925 --> 00:32:08,502
A tank farm.

517
00:32:08,526 --> 00:32:11,072
A tank farm?

518
00:32:11,096 --> 00:32:13,308
Where they store oil.

519
00:32:13,332 --> 00:32:16,044
Only there isn't any
oil at Sorrel Flats.

520
00:32:16,068 --> 00:32:17,178
There isn't?

521
00:32:17,202 --> 00:32:18,813
No, my husband and his friends

522
00:32:18,837 --> 00:32:21,883
have been selling the
government oil that doesn't exist.

523
00:32:21,907 --> 00:32:23,384
You sure?

524
00:32:23,408 --> 00:32:26,276
Are we in the middle of nowhere?

525
00:32:27,913 --> 00:32:29,390
Not anymore.

526
00:32:29,414 --> 00:32:31,681
We're heading for home.

527
00:32:36,755 --> 00:32:39,801
Hello, Michelle.

528
00:32:39,825 --> 00:32:42,804
Now, before you get
excited, this is not an apology.

529
00:32:42,828 --> 00:32:46,140
Then what is it,
another come-on?

530
00:32:46,164 --> 00:32:48,810
Crawford, Fitzmorris,
Irwin, and I,

531
00:32:48,834 --> 00:32:51,746
we were all taking payoffs
from the Leyland Group.

532
00:32:51,770 --> 00:32:53,314
Actually, mine was
more of an annuity,

533
00:32:53,338 --> 00:32:54,782
money they promised
me when I agreed

534
00:32:54,806 --> 00:32:57,385
to run Crawford's
re-election campaign.

535
00:32:57,409 --> 00:32:59,186
What was it for the others?

536
00:32:59,210 --> 00:33:02,690
I don't know. They...

537
00:33:02,714 --> 00:33:04,392
They pulled me over
to Crawford's office

538
00:33:04,416 --> 00:33:05,693
every couple of months.

539
00:33:05,717 --> 00:33:08,663
Fitzmorris would pass out the
cash he got down in Houston,

540
00:33:08,687 --> 00:33:11,933
and we'd all go our separate
ways, no questions asked.

541
00:33:11,957 --> 00:33:13,768
Who was calling the shots?

542
00:33:13,792 --> 00:33:14,969
Irwin.

543
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:16,571
The night of the murder too?

544
00:33:16,595 --> 00:33:19,207
In fact, he called
me that night.

545
00:33:19,231 --> 00:33:20,808
He said that, uh,

546
00:33:20,832 --> 00:33:23,011
Karen had stolen a ledger
from Crawford's desk.

547
00:33:23,035 --> 00:33:24,545
There was nothing
incriminating in it,

548
00:33:24,569 --> 00:33:27,015
but it proved that she was
the one leaking information

549
00:33:27,039 --> 00:33:28,850
to Melinda at the<i> Trib.</i>

550
00:33:28,874 --> 00:33:31,319
He said the others
were at Crawford's office

551
00:33:31,343 --> 00:33:32,553
discussing the situation.

552
00:33:32,577 --> 00:33:34,489
He asked me if I had any ideas.

553
00:33:34,513 --> 00:33:38,293
I said no, and finally hung up.

554
00:33:38,317 --> 00:33:41,385
That still doesn't explain why
you were late to that reception.

555
00:33:43,254 --> 00:33:45,535
I was in bed with
a married woman.

556
00:33:52,364 --> 00:33:54,709
How do I know this is the truth?

557
00:33:54,733 --> 00:33:57,567
It sure as hell isn't a come-on.

558
00:34:03,975 --> 00:34:06,087
No. Absolutely not.

559
00:34:06,111 --> 00:34:08,989
No way. He's not
signing anything.

560
00:34:09,013 --> 00:34:11,725
Not till we get the release.
That's what we agreed on

561
00:34:11,749 --> 00:34:14,028
and that's the way
it's going to stand.

562
00:34:14,052 --> 00:34:16,030
You can tell him I'm sorry.

563
00:34:16,054 --> 00:34:18,232
Are you busy, Mr. Irwin?

564
00:34:18,256 --> 00:34:20,935
Yes. As a matter of fact, I am.

565
00:34:20,959 --> 00:34:24,093
Uh, too busy to say
hello to your wife?

566
00:34:25,597 --> 00:34:27,609
That's right, Robert, darling.

567
00:34:27,633 --> 00:34:30,545
I escaped from that hellhole
that you packed me off to.

568
00:34:30,569 --> 00:34:33,681
If you're responsible
for this, you're through.

569
00:34:33,705 --> 00:34:36,584
Any number of doctors, uh,

570
00:34:36,608 --> 00:34:39,187
will testify that my
wife's mental health

571
00:34:39,211 --> 00:34:41,722
is precarious at best.

572
00:34:41,746 --> 00:34:44,258
He knows about Sorrel Flats.

573
00:34:44,282 --> 00:34:46,361
You know, those big,
waterlogged oil tanks

574
00:34:46,385 --> 00:34:48,729
that belong to all those
rich friends of yours?

575
00:34:48,753 --> 00:34:50,565
Now see what you've done.

576
00:34:50,589 --> 00:34:51,932
She's totally mad.

577
00:34:51,956 --> 00:34:53,868
You're drowning,

578
00:34:53,892 --> 00:34:55,436
and if you think I'm
going to keep lying

579
00:34:55,460 --> 00:34:57,838
about where you were when that
pathetic creature was murdered,

580
00:34:57,862 --> 00:34:59,173
then you're the one who's mad.

581
00:34:59,197 --> 00:35:01,575
Clarissa... He was not home

582
00:35:01,599 --> 00:35:03,044
having dinner
with me that night.

583
00:35:03,068 --> 00:35:07,036
In fact, I'll be damned
if I know where he was.

584
00:35:12,677 --> 00:35:15,289
Uh...

585
00:35:15,313 --> 00:35:18,659
I, uh, I know about
the Leyland Group.

586
00:35:18,683 --> 00:35:24,264
I know about the
fraudulent oil sales.

587
00:35:24,288 --> 00:35:26,868
I know about your meeting
in the senator's office

588
00:35:26,892 --> 00:35:29,726
after Karen stole that ledger.

589
00:35:31,997 --> 00:35:36,110
Is there anything else
I should know about?

590
00:35:40,939 --> 00:35:43,372
Get out.

591
00:36:03,328 --> 00:36:05,106
It's Robert.

592
00:36:05,130 --> 00:36:10,378
Um... It's all coming unraveled.

593
00:36:10,402 --> 00:36:11,812
Yeah.

594
00:36:11,836 --> 00:36:14,471
It's gonna be every
man for himself.

595
00:36:31,256 --> 00:36:33,167
So it was Robert
Irwin that killed her?

596
00:36:33,191 --> 00:36:36,037
Well, I'm not
certain it was him.

597
00:36:36,061 --> 00:36:37,938
What are we looking for?

598
00:36:37,962 --> 00:36:39,707
I don't know.

599
00:36:39,731 --> 00:36:41,375
Maybe the police
overlooked something.

600
00:36:41,399 --> 00:36:43,244
They had Melinda. Maybe
they missed something.

601
00:36:43,268 --> 00:36:45,513
Yeah, but the only fingerprints
they found belonged to her.

602
00:36:45,537 --> 00:36:46,947
Yeah.

603
00:36:46,971 --> 00:36:51,018
Whoever did it did a pretty
good job of cleaning it up.

604
00:36:51,042 --> 00:36:54,922
Well, we know he set the
burglar alarm before he ran.

605
00:36:54,946 --> 00:36:58,125
And he had to have
deactivated it before he entered.

606
00:36:58,149 --> 00:36:59,527
He must've wiped off the panel.

607
00:36:59,551 --> 00:37:02,396
There weren't any prints
found at all, not even hers.

608
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:04,298
Yeah.

609
00:37:04,322 --> 00:37:08,402
Yeah, he did a pretty
good job of cleaning it up.

610
00:37:08,426 --> 00:37:09,559
Hm.

611
00:37:15,433 --> 00:37:18,145
Hm.

612
00:37:18,169 --> 00:37:19,769
Hm.

613
00:37:24,075 --> 00:37:26,954
I wonder if Karen had big hands.

614
00:37:26,978 --> 00:37:29,490
I don't know. Huh.

615
00:37:29,514 --> 00:37:32,827
Looks like a medium or a large.

616
00:37:32,851 --> 00:37:35,730
You think... Well, it might be.

617
00:37:35,754 --> 00:37:39,566
Somebody used these to
clean up after the murder

618
00:37:39,590 --> 00:37:42,503
and then put 'em back in the
bucket where he found 'em.

619
00:37:42,527 --> 00:37:45,072
Now, that is a good theory.

620
00:37:45,096 --> 00:37:48,075
Why don't we take
'em down to the lab

621
00:37:48,099 --> 00:37:51,979
and have a technician turn
them wrong side out real careful?

622
00:37:52,003 --> 00:37:53,514
You want...? Yeah.

623
00:37:53,538 --> 00:37:55,116
Let's take the whole thing.

624
00:37:55,140 --> 00:37:57,039
You got me.

625
00:37:59,778 --> 00:38:02,857
My name is Lambert Crawford.
I'm a United States senator.

626
00:38:02,881 --> 00:38:05,093
I reside in San Antonio, Texas,

627
00:38:05,117 --> 00:38:07,929
and I also have a residence
in the District of Columbia.

628
00:38:07,953 --> 00:38:09,097
Thank you, senator.

629
00:38:09,121 --> 00:38:12,266
For the past ten years,

630
00:38:12,290 --> 00:38:16,337
you've been chairman of a
Senate committee that monitors,

631
00:38:16,361 --> 00:38:19,440
among other things,
federal oil purchases.

632
00:38:19,464 --> 00:38:21,809
Is that correct? Yes, it is.

633
00:38:21,833 --> 00:38:25,779
And the oil the government
purchases from an oil company

634
00:38:25,803 --> 00:38:27,982
called the Leyland Group

635
00:38:28,006 --> 00:38:30,385
is stored at a place
called Sorrel Flats.

636
00:38:30,409 --> 00:38:33,787
Is that correct? I
don't recall offhand.

637
00:38:33,811 --> 00:38:35,790
I'd have to check my records.

638
00:38:35,814 --> 00:38:37,791
And I have to ask of
what possible relevance

639
00:38:37,815 --> 00:38:38,959
is any of this?

640
00:38:38,983 --> 00:38:41,223
I'm establishing
foundation, Your Honor.

641
00:38:43,054 --> 00:38:46,033
The defense contends
that Karen Wyler

642
00:38:46,057 --> 00:38:49,837
was killed because
she was convinced

643
00:38:49,861 --> 00:38:51,839
that you and three other men

644
00:38:51,863 --> 00:38:53,207
were part of a conspiracy.

645
00:38:53,231 --> 00:38:54,709
Are you aware of that, senator?

646
00:38:54,733 --> 00:38:56,310
I've heard that rumor, yes.

647
00:38:56,334 --> 00:38:59,814
Now, isn't it true that
this Leyland Group

648
00:38:59,838 --> 00:39:03,384
made substantial contributions
to your last campaign?

649
00:39:03,408 --> 00:39:07,187
Again, I don't recall offhand,
but I'm sure if they did,

650
00:39:07,211 --> 00:39:08,723
they were legitimate.

651
00:39:08,747 --> 00:39:10,124
Isn't it true they did that

652
00:39:10,148 --> 00:39:12,727
because they were
extremely eager

653
00:39:12,751 --> 00:39:15,096
that you maintain your post

654
00:39:15,120 --> 00:39:16,864
as chairman of that
Senate committee?

655
00:39:16,888 --> 00:39:19,466
All of my political
supportees were interested

656
00:39:19,490 --> 00:39:21,369
in my keeping my Senate posts.

657
00:39:21,393 --> 00:39:24,806
Isn't it true that
this Leyland Group

658
00:39:24,830 --> 00:39:27,108
has sold millions of dollars

659
00:39:27,132 --> 00:39:29,477
of nonexistent oil
to the government?

660
00:39:29,501 --> 00:39:31,012
Not to my knowledge, no.

661
00:39:31,036 --> 00:39:33,213
And isn't it true you
would warn them

662
00:39:33,237 --> 00:39:36,250
whenever government officials
were going to make spot checks

663
00:39:36,274 --> 00:39:37,585
of their storage tanks

664
00:39:37,609 --> 00:39:40,154
so they could move
the existing oil around,

665
00:39:40,178 --> 00:39:44,313
making 3000 barrels
look like 300,000?

666
00:39:46,117 --> 00:39:48,262
I have no knowledge
of anything like that.

667
00:39:48,286 --> 00:39:51,498
And isn't it true that you

668
00:39:51,522 --> 00:39:54,034
and three other men

669
00:39:54,058 --> 00:39:56,804
accepted payoffs for your part

670
00:39:56,828 --> 00:39:59,473
in this very
lucrative conspiracy?

671
00:39:59,497 --> 00:40:02,376
And isn't it true that Karen
Wyler was on the verge

672
00:40:02,400 --> 00:40:03,911
of finding out about it?

673
00:40:03,935 --> 00:40:06,247
And isn't it true that's
why she was killed?

674
00:40:06,271 --> 00:40:08,349
Objection. No foundation.

675
00:40:08,373 --> 00:40:10,050
No relevancy. Speculation.

676
00:40:10,074 --> 00:40:11,619
Sustained.

677
00:40:11,643 --> 00:40:13,220
Mr. Matlock, you
are out of order.

678
00:40:13,244 --> 00:40:15,489
You aren't establishing
foundation here.

679
00:40:15,513 --> 00:40:17,925
You're using this witness
as a excuse to speculate,

680
00:40:17,949 --> 00:40:19,293
and I won't have it.

681
00:40:19,317 --> 00:40:20,828
Either you provide this court

682
00:40:20,852 --> 00:40:22,696
with proof of
what you're saying,

683
00:40:22,720 --> 00:40:25,040
or you move on to a
legitimate line of questioning.

684
00:40:25,923 --> 00:40:27,089
Yes, sir.

685
00:40:29,794 --> 00:40:32,139
We're checking into
your Swiss bank account.

686
00:40:32,163 --> 00:40:33,540
That's not a question.

687
00:40:33,564 --> 00:40:36,644
Matlock, I instruct you to
move on with your questions.

688
00:40:36,668 --> 00:40:39,102
Yes, sir.

689
00:40:45,977 --> 00:40:47,876
Was Karen Wyler your mistress?

690
00:40:49,581 --> 00:40:51,258
She was my secretary.

691
00:40:51,282 --> 00:40:52,793
Now, careful, senator.

692
00:40:52,817 --> 00:40:57,465
Remember those department
store charge cards you gave her?

693
00:40:57,489 --> 00:41:00,167
The bills would come
into the office each month.

694
00:41:00,191 --> 00:41:03,771
You paid out-of-office expenses.

695
00:41:03,795 --> 00:41:05,372
I don't know
anything about this.

696
00:41:05,396 --> 00:41:07,241
She must've done
it behind my back.

697
00:41:07,265 --> 00:41:08,709
How often did you
go to her apartment?

698
00:41:08,733 --> 00:41:09,977
Once a week? Twice a week?

699
00:41:10,001 --> 00:41:11,879
That's not true. Did
you tell her it was over?

700
00:41:11,903 --> 00:41:14,581
Did you cut it off? Is that
why she went to Melinda Stuart

701
00:41:14,605 --> 00:41:16,183
with what she knew
of the conspiracy?

702
00:41:16,207 --> 00:41:17,985
Maybe to get back at you? No.

703
00:41:18,009 --> 00:41:20,854
Is that why you decided
it'd be best for all concerned

704
00:41:20,878 --> 00:41:22,889
if she were dead?
No, that's not true.

705
00:41:22,913 --> 00:41:25,659
I swear it.

706
00:41:25,683 --> 00:41:28,763
I cared about Karen.

707
00:41:28,787 --> 00:41:31,153
I could never kill her.

708
00:41:33,658 --> 00:41:35,970
Never.

709
00:41:37,528 --> 00:41:40,608
That's it for this line of
questioning, Mr. Matlock.

710
00:41:40,632 --> 00:41:43,144
You and your client may
revel in gossip and scandal,

711
00:41:43,168 --> 00:41:45,713
but I assure you,
this court does not.

712
00:41:45,737 --> 00:41:49,750
We're going to take a recess
for lunch now, counselor,

713
00:41:49,774 --> 00:41:52,153
and when we return at 2:00 p.m.,

714
00:41:52,177 --> 00:41:55,389
you will begin to elicit
relevant testimony

715
00:41:55,413 --> 00:41:57,579
and only relevant testimony.

716
00:41:58,783 --> 00:42:01,261
Is that clear?

717
00:42:16,467 --> 00:42:19,479
You had to do it.

718
00:42:19,503 --> 00:42:21,404
I know it.

719
00:42:33,051 --> 00:42:37,553
I sure wish he hadn't
gotten involved in all of this.

720
00:43:10,988 --> 00:43:13,468
Oh.

721
00:43:15,393 --> 00:43:19,306
I guess what the old
folks used to say is right.

722
00:43:19,330 --> 00:43:21,275
You wanna catch a
fellow doing something

723
00:43:21,299 --> 00:43:22,743
he's not supposed to do,

724
00:43:22,767 --> 00:43:25,179
let him catch himself. Hm.

725
00:43:25,203 --> 00:43:26,513
I didn't think it would work.

726
00:43:26,537 --> 00:43:28,515
I figured he'd come in here,

727
00:43:28,539 --> 00:43:30,151
find some way to
set the burglar alarm,

728
00:43:30,175 --> 00:43:32,186
think something was up,
and turn right back around.

729
00:43:32,210 --> 00:43:35,789
Well, desperation.

730
00:43:35,813 --> 00:43:38,158
Desperation and opportunism.

731
00:43:38,182 --> 00:43:41,295
Mr. Fitzmorris here
saw an opportunity

732
00:43:41,319 --> 00:43:45,933
to cash in on what I did to
Senator Crawford in court today.

733
00:43:45,957 --> 00:43:49,403
He saw an opportunity
to frame him real good,

734
00:43:49,427 --> 00:43:52,528
and you weren't about to
let that go by, were you?

735
00:43:53,498 --> 00:43:56,077
Tie clasp.

736
00:43:56,101 --> 00:43:57,544
"L.C."

737
00:43:57,568 --> 00:44:00,602
Lambert Crawford slept here.

738
00:44:01,505 --> 00:44:03,917
He didn't sleep here.

739
00:44:03,941 --> 00:44:06,754
He wasn't having an
affair with Karen. You were.

740
00:44:06,778 --> 00:44:09,923
That's how come you have a key.

741
00:44:09,947 --> 00:44:14,461
That's how come you know
the code to the burglar alarm.

742
00:44:14,485 --> 00:44:16,697
That's absolutely not true.

743
00:44:16,721 --> 00:44:18,232
That's not real proof.

744
00:44:18,256 --> 00:44:23,003
Well, combined
with your fingerprints

745
00:44:23,027 --> 00:44:24,393
in those kitchen gloves...

746
00:44:25,930 --> 00:44:29,877
Kitchen gloves you used
to clean up after the murder.

747
00:44:29,901 --> 00:44:33,602
It casts a long shadow on you.

748
00:44:36,641 --> 00:44:39,252
Has the jury reached a verdict?

749
00:44:39,276 --> 00:44:40,688
We have, Your Honor.

750
00:44:40,712 --> 00:44:44,024
We find the defendant,
Melinda Stuart,

751
00:44:44,048 --> 00:44:45,692
not guilty.

752
00:44:45,716 --> 00:44:47,261
Oh, great.

753
00:44:47,285 --> 00:44:49,596
Thank you.

754
00:44:49,620 --> 00:44:53,834
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

755
00:44:53,858 --> 00:44:56,369
I would expect the
United States Attorney

756
00:44:56,393 --> 00:44:59,273
to conduct a full investigation
into this conspiracy.

757
00:44:59,297 --> 00:45:02,743
This court is adjourned.

758
00:45:02,767 --> 00:45:05,245
Thanks. You're all wonderful.

759
00:45:05,269 --> 00:45:07,447
Thank you. Thank you.

760
00:45:07,471 --> 00:45:09,249
I've gotta get to the
paper. Thanks, again.

761
00:45:09,273 --> 00:45:11,833
Goodbye. MELINDA: Bye.

762
00:45:14,112 --> 00:45:16,123
Congratulations.

763
00:45:16,147 --> 00:45:17,891
Thank you.

764
00:45:17,915 --> 00:45:20,527
And thanks for
leveling with me before.

765
00:45:20,551 --> 00:45:23,063
Maybe I was wrong about you.

766
00:45:23,087 --> 00:45:25,432
No, you weren't.

767
00:45:25,456 --> 00:45:27,400
I just got a phone
call from some people

768
00:45:27,424 --> 00:45:29,036
at the Leyland Group

769
00:45:29,060 --> 00:45:30,938
asking me to help repair
some of the damage

770
00:45:30,962 --> 00:45:33,540
this trial has done to
their corporate image.

771
00:45:33,564 --> 00:45:34,764
Guess what I said?

772
00:45:37,335 --> 00:45:39,068
Good luck.

773
00:45:40,437 --> 00:45:43,150
Mr. Matlock.

774
00:45:43,174 --> 00:45:45,252
Hm. I'm in trouble.

775
00:45:45,276 --> 00:45:47,921
The trial's over, Ben. Not
till he has his last word.

776
00:45:47,945 --> 00:45:49,745
You go ahead.

777
00:45:53,518 --> 00:45:55,929
Yes, sir?

778
00:45:55,953 --> 00:45:59,366
Matlock, you used me.

779
00:45:59,390 --> 00:46:01,735
I don't know what you
mean. Oh, yes, you do.

780
00:46:01,759 --> 00:46:04,805
You knew I'd stop your
examination of Lambert Crawford,

781
00:46:04,829 --> 00:46:06,340
didn't you?

782
00:46:06,364 --> 00:46:08,175
Well, I figured you would.

783
00:46:08,199 --> 00:46:10,644
And you knew the killer
might take this opportunity

784
00:46:10,668 --> 00:46:12,868
to plant new evidence
at the scene of the crime?

785
00:46:15,039 --> 00:46:16,984
Yes, I hoped he would.

786
00:46:17,008 --> 00:46:20,075
And it was all dependent
on me, wasn't it?

787
00:46:21,312 --> 00:46:22,890
Yes.

788
00:46:22,914 --> 00:46:24,112
You used me.

789
00:46:26,317 --> 00:46:27,661
I suppose.

790
00:46:27,685 --> 00:46:31,520
Matlock, I could hate a
man for knowing me that well.

791
00:46:32,757 --> 00:46:35,502
Or you could think
a person like that

792
00:46:35,526 --> 00:46:36,770
was such a fine lawyer,

793
00:46:36,794 --> 00:46:38,394
you'd let him buy you a drink.

794
00:46:39,763 --> 00:46:42,809
You're way out of line.

795
00:46:42,833 --> 00:46:44,912
Hell, it's been a
long day. Hasn't it?

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
